WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.870
This section of the course will go through
the same six types of operators we covered

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:09.920
in the previous topic, but instead of working
on order of operations, we will cover what

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.630
these operators do in detail.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.219
And we will create building blocks for games
to help understand these different types of

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:18.140
operators.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.740
An assignment is a value attached to a variable,
usually with an equal sign.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:29.511
In this 141-assignment code example, we see
a variable named score, on line 1, and it

00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:32.309
is assigned a value of 300.

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:35.260
This assignment is known as a simple assignment.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:41.309
A compound assignment changes the value of
a variable using an arithmetic operator.

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:44.280
Take a look at the code on line 3.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.030
The score +=10 statement adds 10 to the score
and is easier to write than score = score

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.809
+ 10.

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:58.129
Many programming languages support the compound
assignment syntax.

00:00:58.129 --> 00:01:02.350
One benefit of this syntax is one does not
need to type the variable twice, which presents

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.720
an extra opportunity for a typo and an error.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:14.430
I will run this code and, using the print
statement on line 4, we see the current score

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:21.759
of 310, which makes sense, given that we started
with an assignment of 300 to the score variable

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:25.310
and then used a compound operator to add 10
to the score.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.399
Let’s take a moment to test the other common
arithmetic operators on this score variable.

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:39.780
First, I will copy lines 3 and 4 and paste
them three times to create room for three

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.210
more assignments.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:48.890
Get into the habit of reusing code snippets
as much as possible to ease your code building.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:02:02.780
Next, I will change the operators to subtraction,
multiplication, and division and the numbers

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:10.140
to 20, 5, and 10, so we have some variety.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.050
Then, I will run this code.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:18.890
300 + 10 = 310.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.700
Subtract 20, and you get 290.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.440
Multiply by 5, and you get 1450.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.860
Divide by 10, and you get 145.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:34.060
The next operator we will look at in this
topic is the modulus, which is the remainder

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.000
of two numbers divided by each other.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:45.970
I will copy the code from lines 12 and 13
to lines 15 and 16, change the operator to

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:54.890
the modulus operator, a percent symbol, and
change the number to 4.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:03:03.840
When I rerun this code, the rest of the numbers
are the same, and the modulus is 1, since

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.630
145 divided by 4 has a remainder of 1.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:12.730
In gaming, a modulus is often used to see
if any points are left over once points are

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.610
spent on items such as coins or whatever people
obtain in games.

